How AI Assisted Throughout the Implementation

I used AI (Cursor's Composer, then Antigravity/Gemini after hitting Cursor's usage limits) as an incremental pair-programmer rather than a one-shot generator. Nearly every feature was broken into small, scoped prompts with explicit constraints ("don't add X yet," "only touch Y," "don't alter anything else"), which kept the AI from over-building and made each step easy to verify before moving to the next.

Scaffolding and structure. The AI handled repetitive setup reliably: initializing the Vite/React/TypeScript project, stripping boilerplate, and generating empty MVVM file structures (Model/ViewModel/View) for the Home, Favourites, MyPokedex, and Auth pages ahead of any real logic. This staged approach — placeholder files first, then types, then service functions, then model, then hook, then view — kept each layer independently testable and mirrored the MVVM pattern consistently across features.

UI and styling. The header went through several rounds of small, targeted CSS adjustments (colors, active states, button shapes, spacing, star and indicator placement) based on visual feedback rather than specifying the full design up front. The same approach was used for the PokemonCard component: design and layout (favourite star, in-Pokedex indicator, Add to Pokedex button) were built first with explicit "no functionality yet" instructions, and behavior was wired in afterward as a separate step.

API integration and data correctness. For the PokeAPI service, I had the AI define TypeScript interfaces before writing any fetching logic, then implement searchPokemon against those types. When the app initially tried to fetch all 1025 Pokémon on load, load times were too slow, so I redirected the AI to fetch only the 20 Pokémon needed for the current page — a meaningful performance fix driven by testing the app rather than guessing upfront. A data-accuracy bug also came up where Pokémon generation numbers were wrong (e.g. Ogerpon showing as Gen 5 instead of Gen 9); I asked the AI to diagnose the cause and choose the more precise and performant fix, which it did by deriving generation from the already-fetched national Pokédex number instead of an extra network call.

Debugging. AI assistance wasn't error-free. Connecting the Home view once caused a blank-screen bug (importing a TypeScript interface as a runtime value, which Vite couldn't resolve) and, separately, a duplicate search bar. In both cases I asked the AI to diagnose and explain the issue before fixing it, which surfaced the actual root cause instead of just patching symptoms. A similar pattern occurred later when the favourite star button didn't work — the AI explained that PokemonCard was a purely presentational component missing the wiring, then fixed it.

Firebase, Favourites, and My Pokedex. Firebase (Realtime Database, then Authentication) was introduced incrementally: first initialization only, then favourites/pokedex read-write functions, then the corresponding Model/ViewModel/View layers for the Favourites and MyPokedex pages, following the same MVVM constraints already established for Home. Once both pages existed, a follow-up prompt was needed to sync state between them, since a Pokémon's favourite/in-Pokedex status wasn't reflected across both views initially.

Authentication and routing. Auth was added as its own MVVM slice (AuthModel, useAuthViewModel, AuthView) plus a global AuthContext exposing the current user and loading state. Several rounds of prompts fixed UX gaps the AI's first pass missed: unclear error messages for invalid logins, no redirect after signup/login, no login prompt when an unauthenticated user tried to favourite a Pokémon, and no login button in the header. Once auth was stable, I had the AI restructure favourites/Pokedex storage to be scoped per user (users/{userId}/favourites/...) instead of global, updating all the service functions to require a userId while preserving existing behavior.

Overall, AI accelerated repetitive setup, boilerplate, and iteration, but required close, prompt-by-prompt supervision — especially around scope creep, styling judgment calls, performance trade-offs (full fetch vs. paginated fetch), and debugging runtime and state-sync errors that only surfaced after integrating features together.